---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const commands = {
 setup: "python3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install '../prince-model[sbol]'",
 worker: "source .venv/bin/activate\npreflight serve --port 18766 \\\n  --allow-origin http://127.0.0.1:4330 \\\n  --allow-origin http://localhost:4330",
 frontend: "npm ci\nnpm run dev\n# Open http://127.0.0.1:4330",
 cli: "preflight analyze 6f5j --chain C --top 3 \\\n  --hotspots C405,C408 --json analysis.json",
 evidence: 'rows:\n  - residues: [C294]\n    claim: "Replace with the verified claim"\n    source: "10.1073/pnas.1615695114"\n    evidence_class: STRUCTURAL_OBSERVATION\n    direction: SUPPORTS\n    reviewed: false\n    notes: "Verify source, target mapping, and numbering."',
};
---
<Layout title="Quickstart — PRINCE">
  <section class="page-hero wrap"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Prince</a><span>/</span><span>Quickstart</span></div><span class="eyebrow">A short route to your first run</span><h1>Bring a target.<br /><em>Begin here.</em></h1><p class="lede">Run the website and analysis worker on your computer, then follow a real example from structure to result.</p></section>
  <div class="wrap guide-layout"><aside class="guide-nav" aria-label="Guide contents"><span class="eyebrow">On this page</span><a href="#setup">01 · Local setup</a><a href="#first-run">02 · Your first run</a><a href="#inputs">03 · Structure inputs</a><a href="#evidence">04 · Evidence review</a><a href="#exports">05 · Take it with you</a><a href="#troubleshooting">06 · Troubleshooting</a></aside><div class="guide-content">
    <section class="content-section" id="setup"><span class="eyebrow">01 / Local setup</span><h2>A small local <em>workspace.</em></h2><p>From the <code>prince-webapp</code> project directory, use Node 22 and Python 3.10 or later. Install the companion prince-model repository beside this checkout. FreeSASA and Gemmi include native dependencies; a compiler may be needed if a wheel is unavailable.</p><div class="code-block"><header>Install the analysis worker <button data-copy="code-setup">Copy</button></header><pre id="code-setup">{commands.setup}</pre></div><p>Start the worker in one terminal. Keep the origin identical to the address you open in the browser.</p><div class="code-block"><header>Terminal 01 / worker <button data-copy="code-worker">Copy</button></header><pre id="code-worker">{commands.worker}</pre></div><div class="code-block"><header>Terminal 02 / website <button data-copy="code-frontend">Copy</button></header><pre id="code-frontend">{commands.frontend}</pre></div><div class="callout">The default workspace API is <code>http://127.0.0.1:18766</code>. This standalone site uses port 4330; the worker runs separately from the website. Configure an existing worker through Connection settings if needed.</div></section>
    <section class="content-section" id="first-run"><span class="eyebrow">02 / Your first run</span><h2>One example.<br /><em>Two separate answers.</em></h2><p>Open the workspace, confirm the API is online, and use the prefilled example: PDB <code>6F5J</code>, chain <code>C</code>, assembly <code>1</code>, selected hotspots <code>C405,C408</code>.</p><ol class="prose-list"><li>Select the fitted antibody-epitope profile and request three or five sites.</li><li>Choose <strong>Run preflight</strong>. The worker retrieves public coordinates from PDBe and performs real structural analysis.</li><li>Inspect the independent <strong>STOP</strong> for C405/C408. Both residues are unmodelled.</li><li>Inspect the proposed sites and their individual findings. Their verdicts do not replace the selected-hotspot check.</li></ol><a class="button" href="/workspace/">Run the example <span>↗</span></a><div class="code-block"><header>The same check from the CLI <button data-copy="code-cli">Copy</button></header><pre id="code-cli">{commands.cli}</pre></div></section>
    <section class="content-section" id="inputs"><span class="eyebrow">03 / Structure inputs</span><h2>Start from <em>real coordinates.</em></h2><ul class="prose-list"><li><strong>Public entry:</strong> use a four-character PDB ID, such as 6F5J. URLs and server filesystem paths are not accepted as IDs.</li><li><strong>Local structure:</strong> upload a <code>.pdb</code>, <code>.cif</code>, or <code>.mmcif</code> file. Selecting a file replaces the PDB ID.</li><li><strong>Size:</strong> the entire JSON request must fit within 8 MiB, including escaped structure text and evidence. One model and no more than 100,000 atoms are accepted.</li><li><strong>Identity:</strong> chain and hotspot inputs use deposited author identifiers. No numbering offsets are guessed.</li><li><strong>Assembly:</strong> leave blank when no biological assembly is defined. Choose the relevant assembly when it is available.</li></ul><p>Uploaded structures are sent to the configured API only when you submit. The supported worker processes them locally in temporary files.</p></section>
    <section class="content-section" id="evidence"><span class="eyebrow">04 / Evidence review</span><h2>Keep the source.<br /><em>Do the reading.</em></h2><p>Open the Source excerpts section in the workspace, supply a DOI or PMID, and paste text you are authorized to use. After analysis, inspect and download the unreviewed drafts.</p><p>Read the original source and verify target or homologue mapping, author numbering, assay type, citation, and direction. Only after that review should a row receive <code>reviewed: true</code> and an attributable <code>reviewer</code>.</p><div class="code-block"><header>Schema example / awaiting source review <button data-copy="code-evidence">Copy</button></header><pre id="code-evidence">{commands.evidence}</pre></div><p>Upload reviewed YAML under Reviewed evidence. Choose evidence-first ranking only if it contains qualifying reviewed, attributed functional-assay or mutagenesis coverage. An unsupported evidence-first request returns a visible error.</p><div class="callout warning-callout">The deterministic local extractor prepares drafts for human source review. Missing coverage leaves the site's functional evidence unknown.</div></section>
    <section class="content-section" id="exports"><span class="eyebrow">05 / Take it with you</span><h2>Keep the whole <em>record.</em></h2><p><strong>JSON</strong> preserves the full response and provenance. <strong>CSV</strong> includes proposed sites, scores, evidence, findings, and the selected-hotspot verdict. <strong>YAML</strong> preserves source-grounded drafts for review.</p><p>Retain the model and structural provenance when comparing runs. Raw structural scores may be negative and should not be labeled as probabilities.</p></section>
    <section class="content-section" id="troubleshooting"><span class="eyebrow">06 / Troubleshooting</span><h2>A few common <em>snags.</em></h2><div class="faq-list"><details><summary>The API is unavailable. <span>+</span></summary><p>Start the worker, open Connection settings, and confirm the API URL and port. The browser origin must match an allowed origin exactly. localhost and 127.0.0.1 are different origins.</p></details><details><summary>Public PDB retrieval failed. <span>+</span></summary><p>Confirm the entry ID and network connection. The worker fetches coordinates from the fixed PDBe endpoint with a 20-second timeout. You can upload a local coordinate file instead.</p></details><details><summary>My upload fits, but the request is too large. <span>+</span></summary><p>The 8 MiB cap applies to the serialized JSON body, including escaping and evidence. Use a smaller structure, reduce source text, or use the CLI for local-file analysis.</p></details><details><summary>Can I put the Python worker on a public server? <span>+</span></summary><p>The supplied worker is designed for loopback use. Public hosting requires additional authentication, authorization, rate limiting, and job orchestration. A static frontend host cannot execute this Python worker.</p></details></div></section>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
